  "markdown": "## Business Model\nCostco operates membership warehouses and e-commerce sites based on the concept of offering low prices on a limited selection of nationally-branded and private-label products [10-K line 00134]. The company emphasizes high sales volumes and rapid inventory turnover, achieved through volume purchasing, efficient distribution, and reduced handling in no-frills, self-service warehouse facilities [10-K line 00134].\n\nThe business model is built on a membership format where annual fees are collected, with the majority of members being paid members who can utilize their memberships at all warehouses and e-commerce sites [10-K line 00157]. Executive members pay an additional fee and earn rewards redeemable at Costco warehouses [10-K line 00166].\n\nThe company's warehouses operate with shorter hours than many other retailers, leading to lower labor costs relative to sales volume due to operational efficiencies [10-K line 00140]. Merchandise is stored on racks above the sales floor and displayed in large quantities, reducing labor requirements [10-K line 00140].\n\nCostco's core merchandise categories include foods and sundries, non-foods, fresh foods, warehouse ancillary (gasoline, pharmacy, food court), and other businesses such as e-commerce, business centers, travel, and others [10-K line 00143].\n\n## Competitive Position\nCostco competes globally with a wide range of wholesalers and retailers including supermarkets, supercenters, online retailers, gasoline stations, hard discounters, department and specialty stores, and warehouse clubs such as Sam’s Club and BJ’s Wholesale Club [10-K line 00190].\n\nThe company has significant competitive advantages rooted in its low-price strategy, efficient operations, strong brand recognition, and private-label offerings like Kirkland Signature [10-K line 00192]. The membership format reinforces member loyalty and provides a steady revenue stream through annual fees [10-K line 00157].\n\nCostco's membership renewal rates are high: 92.3% in the U.S. and Canada, and 89.8% worldwide at the end of 2026 [10-K line 00167]. This reflects strong customer retention and satisfaction.\n\n## Growth Drivers\nKey growth drivers include increases in comparable sales, driven by higher shopping frequency and average ticket size [10-K line 00451]. Comparable sales were positively impacted by a 5% increase in average ticket and 3% in shopping frequency during 2026 [10-K line 00451].\n\nNew warehouse openings contribute to growth, with 28 new warehouses opened in 2026, including relocations [10-K line 00415]. Sales from new warehouses were a significant contributor to overall net sales growth [10-K line 00451].\n\nDigital sales also play an important role in growth, with digitally-enabled comparable sales increasing 21% in 2026 [10-K line 00452].\n\nMembership fee revenue increases from new sign-ups, fee hikes, and upgrades to Executive memberships have been a key driver [10-K line 00458].\n\n## Catalysts\nCatalysts for future performance include continued expansion of the membership base, which contributed to membership fee revenue growth [10-K line 00458].\n\nThe company's e-commerce operations continue to grow, offering convenience and broader selections that complement warehouse operations [10-K line 00153].\n\nThe company’s ability to manage costs effectively, including through employee compensation strategies and leveraging economies of scale, remains a key catalyst [10-K line 00407].\n\n## Recent Developments\nIn 2026, Costco opened 28 new warehouses (including three relocations) [10-K line 00415]. Net sales increased by 10% to $297.2 billion, driven by comparable sales growth and new warehouse contributions [10-K line 00416].\n\nMembership fee revenue rose 11% to $5.9 billion, supported by new member sign-ups, fee increases, and Executive upgrades [10-K line 00418].\n\nGross margin percentage decreased three basis points, though it increased 10 basis points excluding the impact of gasoline price inflation [10-K line 00419].\n\nSG&A expenses as a percentage of net sales decreased 10 basis points [10-K line 00420].\n\nIn April 2026, the Board approved a 13% increase in the quarterly cash dividend [10-K line 00423].\n\nIEEPA tariff refunds of $184 were received during 2026, with $174 recorded as a reduction to merchandise costs and $10 as interest income [10-K line 00426].\n\n## Material Risks\nCostco faces risks related to global economic conditions, currency fluctuations, and inflation or deflation affecting gasoline prices [10-K line 00398].\n\nThe company is exposed to tariffs, with some merchandise being affected by U.S. tariffs under the International Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA), although a ruling in February 2026 made those tariffs unlawful [10-K line 00425].\n\nRising costs for employee compensation and benefits are a concern, although Costco’s philosophy is to maintain compensation levels above industry averages to reduce turnover and increase productivity [10-K line 00407].\n\nCompetition remains intense across multiple retail formats including online and physical stores, which can affect net sales and gross margins [10-K line 00190].\n\nThere is risk associated with the company's reliance on a limited number of suppliers for some merchandise and the potential for supply disruptions [10-K line 00154].",
